      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Pulse Slate 3 · short path (one window, ≤ 15s)\n\nThis graph compiles **and** samples in the open, with your own sampler nodes.\nNothing is muted. For anything longer than one window, open\n**PulseSlate_LongForm.json** instead — it wires `timeline → Pulse Render`.\n\n## What changed in 3.0.0\n`PulseSlate` no longer renders. It compiles a **PULSE_TIMELINE** and hands back\n`positive` + `latent` *only while the timeline fits in one window*. Past that it\nblocks those two outputs and tells you to add a **Pulse Render** node, instead of\nhanding back the last window alone — which is what silently produced a 7-second\nfile from a 15-second timeline in 2.x.\n\n`model` is no longer an output. Sigma shift is a model patch like any other, so\nit belongs upstream: **UNETLoader → MiniMaxH3SigmaShift → your sampler**.\n\n## Where you type\n- **GLOBAL PROMPT** — style, identity locks, score. Line labels: `style:`\n  `identity:` `retention:` `soundscape:` `music:`\n- **SHOT PROMPT** — one shot per line, `[Shot N]` or `[MM:SS.mmm]`.\n\nOr wire **Pulse Shot** nodes into the `shots.shot_*` sockets. The moment one is\nconnected the text box is ignored outright — never merged — and the compiled\nprompt says so at the top.\n\n## Never type a tag number\nReference bin assets by **name**: `@Image1`, `@Mimi`, `@[Cafe wide]`.\n`<Picture 3>` is assigned from bin order at compile time and shifts whenever the\nbin changes. A hand-typed number describes the wrong asset and still renders\nsuccessfully. Read `compiled_prompt` — its ordinal map is the check.\n\n## cfg\nLeave at **1.0**. H3's own pipeline uses BasicGuider and has no negative\nconditioning anywhere.\n"
      ],
      "title": "READ ME"
    }
